    "passage": "Located in Eastern Greenland, one of the most remote places from civilization, the town of Ittoqqortoormiit is the only inhabited part of the land on the rugged and beautiful coastline south of Greenland National Park. Greenland’s population density is an incomprehensible 0.0 people per-square-kilometer, and three-quarters of Greenland’s 57,000 inhabitants live in the capital city, Nuuk, on the west coast. [[1]], there is barely any human residence for a 1,000-kilometer radius around Ittoqqortoormiit.\nDeserted [[2]] it is, the town is full of brightly painted houses. At the center of this tiny village [[3]] a bright orange guest house which residents hope will attract tourism in a remote corner of the world’s most isolated country. The well-heated guest house with wooden floors, a well-stocked refrigerator and a bunch of 90s DVDs is probably the world’s most remote hotel, easily beating other [[4]] in Australia, Chile and Mongolia.\nIn this remote area, the locals still have access to basic infrastructure. The people of Ittoqqortoormiit, all of [[5]] are of Inuit descent, have electricity, central heating and Wi-Fi at the local recreation center. They also shop online from Amazon, only that parcels arrive by a charter plane once every two months from Iceland.",
